# =============================================================================
# primus_quem (AIR lane) — bounded-source admission rows.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • the head admits on the AIR lane only over statically bounded sources
#     (Route T literal array; Route M statically shaped tensor, including
#     the zero-extent shape) — admission needs the bound, not an unroll
#   • a named AIR predicate (`serva`) is admitted as the `ubi` tail; the head
#     is not a method, so no method-allowlist row is involved
#   • a statically zero-extent source is a bounded route and admits; the
#     `nihil` contract is carried unchanged through the gate
#   • reverse AD is forward-only v1: the head never becomes an AIR tensor op
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting unbounded `lista` parameters to admit — they decline with
#     `first_match_unbounded_source` (see primus-quem-decline.fab)
#   • expecting `apud` coordinates to admit on the AIR lane — the
#     multi-axis form stays unsupported in v1
#
# See also: primus_quem, ubi, tensor
# =============================================================================

# Named AIR predicate for the ubi tail.
@ radix lane "air"
functio serva(fractus x) → bivalens {
    redde x ≻ 0.0
}

# Route M — statically shaped tensor parameter admits with a first-live mask.
@ radix lane "air"
functio elige(tf32[4] x) → fractus ∪ nihil {
    redde primus_quem(x, ubi v { serva(v) })
}

# Route M, zero extent — a statically empty source admits; result is nihil.
@ radix lane "air"
functio elige_vacuum(tf32[0] x) → fractus ∪ nihil {
    redde primus_quem(x, ubi v { v ≻ 0.0 })
}

# Route T — per-element array literal (no spread) admits.
@ radix lane "air"
functio elige_litteram() → fractus ∪ nihil {
    redde primus_quem([1.0, -2.0, 3.0], ubi v { v ≻ 0.0 })
}
